<!--
  scouting — watching-zenoh pre-session Scout/Hello discovery FSM
  (active mode). Authored per docs/scouting-fsm.md §10 next-step
  scaffolding ("Phase A SCXML authoring of sources/session/scouting.scxml
  for {active, static}").

  This is a statechart-kind SCXML, the same engine-free shape as
  network/reassembly_slot.scxml: sce:kind="statechart" with native
  `<sce:action name=>` host-trait calls (no IScriptEngine / Lua), so the
  generated ScoutingPolicy carries no script engine and the module
  compiles #![no_std]. It is codegen'd in no-std mode by the sce-codegen
  CLI (see crates/wz-session-core/build.rs STATECHARTS) into
  $OUT_DIR/scouting_sm.rs and included behind
  #[cfg(feature = "scouting-active")].

  Timeout ownership (engine-free no_std, mirrors reassembly_slot.scxml
  §"Timeout ownership"): under no-std codegen the generated machine binds
  `type Hal = NoOpHal` (now_ticks_ms() == 0), so a W3C SCXML `<send
  delay>` is emitted but DEAD — its scheduled event can never fire
  because the no_std clock never advances. A self-armed timer in the FSM
  would therefore be a non-functional statechart element. So this FSM
  arms NO timer of its own: it owns the `scout.timer.elapsed` TRANSITION
  only (AwaitingHello -> Idle), and the host drive loop owns the CLOCK,
  raising scout.timer.elapsed when the deadline elapses. The deadline
  VALUE stays spec-sourced (scouting.timeout_ms, docs/scouting-fsm.md
  §2.5, carried in ScoutParams), so SSOT is preserved: value = spec,
  transition = statechart, clock = runtime — each owned by the right
  layer. This is the same clock/transition split reassembly uses for its
  buffer-pool reassembly.timeout.

  Position. Pre-session. Turns "I need to find a peer to handshake
  with" into a concrete unicast locator the session FSM
  (session_fsm_unicast.scxml) drives Init -> LinkOpening -> Opening
  against. Emits the discovered locators upward via record_hello_and_emit;
  never opens a session, never handshakes (docs/scouting-fsm.md §1.1).

  Scope = active mode only. Per docs/scouting-fsm.md §2.4.3 the static
  mode never instantiates the scouting FSM (the host synthesizes the
  locator from deploy.connect[] at startup); passive mode is deferred to
  Phase D+ (OQ-W23). So this statechart carries the single mode whose
  body is grounded in zenoh-pico file:line evidence (__z_scout_loop,
  vendor/zenoh-pico/src/session/scout.c:29-140).

  Active body = __z_scout_loop lifted into states per the per-line
  mapping in docs/scouting-fsm.md §2.4.1:
    - Idle->Sending scout_emit          <- scout.c:57 _z_link_send_wbuf
    - AwaitingHello (host clock)        <- scout.c:60-63 RX setup + clock
                                           + while elapsed < period
    - AwaitingHello hello.received      <- scout.c:80-119 _Z_MID_HELLO arm
    - exit_on_first short-circuit       <- scout.c:121-123, BOTH arms

  exit_on_first is a PARAMETER upstream, not a hardcode, and both of its
  values ship: `_z_scout_inner(.., exit_on_first)` is called with `true`
  by the session-open implicit scout (`src/net/session.c:69`, which needs
  one dial target) and with `false` by the public `z_scout` survey API
  (`src/net/primitives.c:81`, which reports every responder). This FSM
  carries both, guarded natively on the typed `_event.data.exit_on_first`
  field of `hello_received_schema.scxml`: exit-on-first leaves for Idle,
  collecting self-transitions and lets the host's timeout close the
  window — which is exactly what pico's `break` vs "keep looping" does.

  Host actions (the generated ScoutingActions trait, one method per
  distinct `<sce:action name>`; impl'd by the host in scouting_glue.rs):
    scout_emit            — encode Scout(what=3, local_zid) + stage the
                            datagram for the drive loop to send on the
                            udp_scout multicast link.
    record_hello_and_emit — record the staged Hello (every field of it)
                            + capture the discovered locator for the
                            session-open caller.
    emit_scout_timeout    — record "no peer found within timeout".
    diag_scout_tx_failed  — observability: the scout frame TX failed.
  All four are no-arg: they read/write host staging slots via &mut self,
  not `_event.data` (the loop owns the socket + the staging slots — cf.
  the IO-ownership note in scouting_glue.rs). The one event-schema import
  below exists for a transition GUARD, not for an action; the actions
  still take nothing.

  Events fed by the host runtime:
    session_open.requested — application/SCE asks to discover a peer.
    scout.tx.done          — host completed the multicast Scout send.
    link.tx_failed         — the scouting link TX errored.
    hello.received         — host decoded a Hello on the scouting link.
                             Carries the typed `_event.data` payload of
                             hello_received_schema.scxml (exit_on_first);
                             the host re-states the cycle's mode on every
                             raise because the engine-free surface has no
                             datamodel to hold it.
    scout.timer.elapsed    — host drive loop's deadline (scouting.timeout_ms,
                             ScoutParams) elapsed with no Hello; raised by
                             the loop, not a self-armed <send> (see
                             "Timeout ownership" above).

  Trust class. The scouting link is `untrusted` (docs/scouting-fsm.md
  §1.3); it can never drive a session handshake. This FSM only produces
  locators — the session FSM's session_arming trust gate is the
  mechanical defense, so no trust assertion is needed here.
-->
<scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml"
       xmlns:sce="http://sce.dev/ext"
       xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:schemaLocation="http://sce.dev/ext sce-forge.xsd"
       sce:kind="statechart"
       name="scouting"
       version="1.0"
       initial="Idle">

  <!-- The typed `_event.data` channel for `hello.received`. Carries the
       one field a native guard needs: the cycle's exit_on_first mode. -->
  <sce:import src="hello_received_schema.scxml" kind="event-schema" as="HelloReceived"/>

  <!-- Waiting for a session-open trigger. Re-entered after every scout
       cycle (active mode is one-shot per request). scout_emit runs on the
       entering transition (not Sending.onentry): `<sce:action>` is a
       `<transition>`-child only in the engine-free SCE surface (R311ij),
       and Sending has exactly one entry path (this transition), so a
       transition action is the exact equivalent of an onentry action. -->
  <state id="Idle">
    <transition event="session_open.requested" target="Sending">
      <sce:action name="scout_emit"/>
    </transition>
  </state>

  <!-- Encode + transmit one Scout frame on the udp_scout link.
       scout.c:57 _z_link_send_wbuf maps to scout_emit on the entering
       transition above. -->
  <state id="Sending">
    <transition event="scout.tx.done" target="AwaitingHello"/>
    <transition event="link.tx_failed" target="Idle">
      <sce:action name="diag_scout_tx_failed"/>
    </transition>
  </state>

  <!-- Poll the scouting link RX path for Hello replies until the host
       drive loop's scouting.timeout_ms deadline elapses (scout.c:60-63).
       The FSM arms no timer of its own (engine-free no_std: a <send
       delay> would bind NoOpHal and never fire — see "Timeout ownership"
       in the header); the host loop owns the clock and raises
       scout.timer.elapsed at the deadline.

       The two hello.received arms are pico's `if (!empty && exit_on_first)
       break;` (scout.c:121-123) split into the statechart. The implicit
       scout (exit_on_first != 0) records the Hello and returns to Idle,
       which is what ends the host's drive loop. The survey arm
       (exit_on_first == 0) records it and RE-ENTERS AwaitingHello, so the
       window keeps running and later responders are recorded too; only
       scout.timer.elapsed closes that window. AwaitingHello has no
       onentry / onexit content, so the self-transition is observationally
       an internal one — the same shape reassembly_slot.scxml's
       Receiving -> Receiving continuation arm uses.

       Both arms run record_hello_and_emit: pico pushes the hello to its
       list BEFORE consulting exit_on_first, so the flag decides only
       whether the loop keeps going, never whether the peer is recorded. -->
  <state id="AwaitingHello">
    <transition event="hello.received" cond="_event.data.exit_on_first !== 0" target="Idle">
      <sce:action name="record_hello_and_emit"/>
    </transition>
    <transition event="hello.received" cond="_event.data.exit_on_first === 0" target="AwaitingHello">
      <sce:action name="record_hello_and_emit"/>
    </transition>
    <transition event="scout.timer.elapsed" target="Idle">
      <sce:action name="emit_scout_timeout"/>
    </transition>
  </state>

</scxml>
